<main>
  <h1>prove.my</h1>
  <p><strong>An anonymizing KYC broker.</strong></p>
  <p>
    prove.my sits between the many government age and driving-licence services
    and the many operators that need to gate a regulated action. A human
    authorizes prove.my once; it returns to the requesting operator a
    <strong>signed, anonymized, single-use</strong> claim — "over 18",
    "holds a category-A licence" — and <strong>nothing that identifies the
    person</strong>: not the name, date of birth, or licence number.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each operator trusts prove.my as an issuer once and asks for exactly the
    facts it needs. The operator never registers with a government identity
    service — prove.my does that, once, for everyone.
  </p>

  <p class="stub">
    <strong>Demo stub.</strong> This is not a Kiosk operator: it exposes none of
    the four Kiosk verbs. The human verification page below self-asserts the
    facts (you click yes/no). In production prove.my reads them from a
    government identity service (e.g. an mDL / EUDI wallet) you log into — so
    you cannot answer for someone else. It proves the <em>protocol</em>
    (per-request binding, no replay, anti-mass-confirm, signed anonymized
    callback), not that the human is actually over 18.
  </p>

  <p class="stub">
    Verifications in flight: <%= @pending %> pending, <%= @confirmed %> confirmed.
    The verification page is reached only from an operator-issued link.
  </p>
</main>
